Apache Spark is a unified, distributed analytics engine designed for large-scale data processing.
The fundamental reason it replaced Hadoop MapReduce as the industry standard comes down to one core architectural decision: Spark processes data in RAM (in-memory),
whereas MapReduce constantly reads and writes intermediate data to physical disk.

Technical Breakdown :-
In-Memory Processing vs. Disk I/O: In MapReduce, every single map and reduce operation requires writing the intermediate results back to the hard drive (HDFS) and reading them again for the next step.
Spark keeps the data in memory across multiple transformations, effectively eliminating the massive latency of disk I/O.

The DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph): MapReduce forces you into a rigid, two-step paradigm: Map, then Reduce.
If you have a complex job, you have to chain multiple jobs together. Spark builds a DAG of your entire logical pipeline,
optimizes it under the hood (using the Catalyst Optimizer), and executes it dynamically.

API Usability: Writing MapReduce required hundreds of lines of verbose Java code.
Spark provides highly expressive APIs in Python (PySpark), Scala, and standard SQL.

Buisness Context :-
From a business perspective, Spark is about 'time-to-insight'.
For simple, single-pass batch jobs, Spark is about 10x faster than MapReduce.
But for iterative workloads—like training a machine learning model where the algorithm has to pass over the exact same dataset hundreds of times—Spark can be 100x faster,
taking processes from days down to minutes.

Design Decision :-
While Spark definitively won the compute war over MapReduce,
the way we use it has evolved. I rarely stand up raw Apache Spark clusters on EC2 instances anymore.
Instead, I rely on managed, serverless platforms like Databricks or AWS EMR.
More importantly, we've fully decoupled compute from storage.
MapReduce was tightly coupled to HDFS storage on the same physical machines; today,
we spin up ephemeral Spark compute clusters that process data sitting cheaply and independently in cloud object storage like S3.
